```php
class AdminRecipients
{
    protected static bool $roleLoaded = false;
    protected static ?Role $adminRole = null;

    public function get(): Collection
    {
        if (!static::$roleLoaded) {
            static::$adminRole = Role::query()->where('system_name', '=', 'admin')->first();
            static::$roleLoaded = true;
        }

        return static::$adminRole?->users()->get() ?? new Collection();
    }
}
```

- **One lookup per request:** the role is stored in static properties. There's a separate `$roleLoaded` flag so that "no admin role" is also remembered. I didn't use the existing `Role::getSystemRole()` because its `isset` check treats a missing role as not cached and runs the query again on every call.
- **Users aren't cached:** only the role is reused. The list of users is fetched fresh on each `get()` call, so it stays current.
- **Tests:** static values last for the whole PHP process, which means across tests in one PHPUnit run too. That's fine as long as the admin role's ID doesn't change between tests. If it does, the class could be registered as a scoped binding in the service container instead.

